0:{"rsc":["$","$1","c",{"children":[[["$","$L2","3.5",{"topic":{"id":"3.5","title":"Deep Web: Concepts and Investigation Methods","minutes":10,"summary":"The deep web is content not indexed by ordinary search engines; it is not a synonym for criminal activity or the dark web.","sections":[{"title":"Understanding non-indexed content","body":["Search engines discover and index a subset of accessible web content. Pages behind logins, dynamic database responses, private dashboards, paywalls, and pages excluded from crawlers may be part of the deep web. Ordinary email inboxes, bank portals, and internal business systems illustrate why non-indexed does not mean illicit.","A page’s absence from a search result can have many causes: authentication, robots exclusion, crawling limitations, recent publication, or technical errors. It does not establish deliberate concealment. “Deep web investigation” therefore begins with identifying the access mechanism and the lawful authority to use it."]},{"title":"Authorized access and reliable exports","body":["For a complainant’s own account, use supported export tools and preserve the original response, headers, and relevant metadata. For organizational systems, define the account, role, tenant, and date range. A screenshot of a dashboard may not show filters or time-zone settings that materially change interpretation.","Do not bypass authentication, exploit a vulnerability, or reuse leaked credentials as an OSINT shortcut. If records are relevant but inaccessible, seek consent, provider assistance, or competent legal process. Record what the export includes and excludes, especially for paginated or dynamically loaded content."]},{"title":"From private records to investigative leads","body":["Authenticated records can be highly probative because they connect activity to account sessions, roles, and business processes. Yet logs can reflect automated actions or administrator impersonation. Corroborate important events with audit trails, payment records, and independent context. Preserve the query and export settings.","Where a lawful investigation leads from a public page to a login-protected resource, stop at the boundary and reassess authority. The technical ability to guess an identifier is not permission to retrieve another person’s information. Explain this boundary in the report so the reader understands why collection stopped."]}],"practice":{"title":"Export an authorized dashboard","scenario":"A shop owner consents to export their own store’s order history for a disputed transaction.","steps":["Confirm the owner’s control and define the transaction date range.","Record filters, time zone, and export format.","Preserve the original export and calculate its hash.","Identify backend records that only the provider can supply."],"debrief":"Deep-web material can often be collected through ordinary lawful account access. The export’s scope and provenance matter more than the label."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["it-act","bnss"],"sources":["itact","nist-guide","privacy"],"unit":3,"objectives":["Explain understanding non-indexed content and apply it to an investigation.","Explain authorized access and reliable exports and apply it to an investigation.","Explain from private records to investigative leads and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-3.5","prompt":"Which is a typical deep-web example?","options":["A public news homepage indexed by search engines.","An authenticated bank account dashboard.","Every page on the Tor network.","A printed newspaper."],"answer":1,"explanation":"The deep web includes non-indexed content such as authenticated dashboards; it is broader than anonymity networks.","unit":3}}}]],["$L3"],"$L4"]}],"isPartial":false,"staleTime":300,"varyParams":null,"buildId":"mMiCr23A_-Cpk0hZkfT7X"}
